---
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { SITE } from '../constants/site';
import { STRINGS } from '../constants/strings';
import { formatDate } from '../data/format';
import { atlas } from '../data/load';
import * as route from '../data/routes';
/**
 * Attribution and disclaimers.
 *
 * Three distinct disclaimers live here and each one is doing different work:
 *   1. This is not an official edition of anything.
 *   2. The eCFR itself is not the official CFR — an easy thing for a reader to get wrong when
 *      the interface looks authoritative.
 *   3. The word counts are this project's own derived measurements and are published by no
 *      government body.
 * Collapsing them into one sentence would lose the third, which is the one this site most needs
 * to make.
 *
 * The essay paragraphs stay inline while the headings and the callout titles live in STRINGS.
 * Each of these sentences wraps a link, a `<strong>` or an `<em>` around its own middle, and a
 * disclaimer split into fragments is both unreadable here and untranslatable there.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const { manifest } = await atlas();
---

<Base
  title={STRINGS.about.title}
  description={STRINGS.about.description}
  section="about"
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow={STRINGS.about.eyebrow}
    title={STRINGS.about.heading}
    standfirst={STRINGS.about.standfirst}
  />

  <div class="prose-page">
    <h2>{STRINGS.about.headings.whatThisIs}</h2>
    <p>
      eCFR Atlas measures the Code of Federal Regulations and publishes the results: how many
      words each agency's regulations run to, where jurisdiction overlaps, how often each part
      changes, and the full text of every part with a citation you can copy. It is optimised for
      lookup, citation and export rather than for browsing.
    </p>
    <p>
      It exists because the obvious version of this site is easy to build wrongly. Its predecessor
      published word counts it had inferred rather than measured, and stored them in the same
      place as the real ones — so “we could not measure this” and “we measured 104,642” became
      indistinguishable a layer downstream. Everything about the current design, down to the
      shape of the database columns, is arranged so that cannot recur.
    </p>

    <h2>{STRINGS.about.headings.dataSource}</h2>
    <p>
      All source material comes from the <strong>Electronic Code of Federal Regulations</strong>
      public API at <a href={SITE.ecfrHomeUrl} rel="external noopener">ecfr.gov</a>. The eCFR
      is a joint project of the <strong>Office of the Federal Register</strong> (National Archives
      and Records Administration) and the <strong>Government Publishing Office</strong>. Related
      material is published through <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov" rel="external noopener">
      govinfo.gov</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Federal government works are not subject to copyright in the United States. This project
      adds measurements, structure and presentation; the underlying regulatory text belongs to the
      public.
    </p>
    <p>
      {
        manifest.fixture
          ? STRINGS.about.buildFromFixture
          : manifest.source_date
            ? STRINGS.about.buildAsOf(formatDate(manifest.source_date))
            : STRINGS.about.buildDateUnknown
      }
    </p>

    <h2>{STRINGS.about.headings.disclaimers}</h2>

    <Callout tone="unknown" title={STRINGS.about.disclaimer1Title}>
      <p>
        eCFR Atlas is an independent open-source project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by,
        sponsored by or an official publication of the United States Government, the National
        Archives and Records Administration, the Office of the Federal Register or the Government
        Publishing Office.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout tone="unknown" class="mt-3" title={STRINGS.about.disclaimer2Title}>
      <p>
        The eCFR is an editorial compilation maintained for the public's convenience and is
        <em>not</em> an official legal edition of the Code of Federal Regulations. Only the annual
        printed CFR and the daily Federal Register are authoritative for legal purposes. If a
        deadline or a legal obligation turns on the exact text, verify it against those.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout tone="unknown" class="mt-3" title={STRINGS.about.disclaimer3Title}>
      <p>
        No government body publishes a word count of the CFR. Every count on this site is a
        <strong>derived measurement produced by this project's own parser</strong>, under the rules
        set out on <a href={route.methodology()}>methodology</a>. They are reproducible and the
        code is open, but they are not official statistics and should not be cited as such. Cite
        them as eCFR Atlas measurements, with the source date.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Where a value could not be measured, this site shows an em dash and the reason — never a
        zero. What is missing is enumerated on <a href={route.dataQuality()}>data quality</a>.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2>{STRINGS.about.headings.citing}</h2>
    <p>
      For a figure: <em>eCFR Atlas, “{`{page title}`}”, measurement current as of
      {manifest.source_date ? ` ${manifest.source_date}` : ' {source date}'}, {SITE.canonicalOrigin.replace('https://', '')}.</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      For regulation text: cite the CFR itself, and verify against the official sources above. The
      copy-citation control on every part page produces a citation with the eCFR link and the
      source date already in it.
    </p>

    <h2>{STRINGS.about.headings.privacy}</h2>
    <p>
      This site is static HTML served from a CDN. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics, embeds no
      third-party scripts, and loads no external fonts or images. The only thing it stores in your
      browser is your light/dark theme preference, and only if you change it from the default.
    </p>

    <h2>{STRINGS.about.headings.contributing}</h2>
    <p>
      The pipeline, the API and this site are open source. Corrections to the measurement rules are
      especially welcome: if you think the tokenisation or the exclusion list is wrong, the
      argument is worth having in public. See <a href={route.apiPage()}>the API page</a> for
      programmatic access and <a href={route.methodology()}>methodology</a> for the current rules.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
